The fix is one character. Using `<` stops the loop at the last index that actually exists.

```java
int[] a = {10, 20, 30};
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) { // i goes 0, 1, 2
    System.out.println(a[i]);
}
```
Arrays are zero-indexed, so for `n` elements the valid indices run from `0` to `n - 1`. The pattern `i < length` is the muscle memory that keeps you on the right side of that boundary every time.

Seeing all three side by side makes the decision rule concrete: the `for` knows its count, the `while` waits on external state, and the `do-while` insists on running once.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class LoopComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // for: known iteration count
        var orders = List.of("Order1", "Order2", "Order3");
        for (int i = 0; i < orders.size(); i++) {
            System.out.println("Processing: " + orders.get(i));
        }

        // while: condition checked first (read until none left)
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner("line1\nline2");
        while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
            System.out.println("Read: " + scanner.nextLine());
        }

        // do-while: always runs once (show menu)
        String choice;
        do {
            System.out.println("Menu: 1-Start 2-Exit");
            choice = "2"; // simulated input
        } while (!choice.equals("2"));
    }
}
```
Notice the `do-while` prints the menu before `choice` is ever compared — that single guaranteed pass is the behaviour you'd have to fake with duplicated code if you used a `while` instead.

When you're nested two loops deep and want to bail out completely, a plain `break` quietly does only half the job — and the outer loop keeps grinding.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (i + j == 2) break; // exits inner loop only — outer keeps going
    }
}
```
The `break` lands you back in the outer loop, which immediately starts the next `i` and re-enters the inner loop. You think you've left, but you're still iterating — a subtle bug when the inner work has side effects.

Java lets you tag a loop with a label and aim `break` at it directly, jumping clean out of both loops in one statement.

```java
outer:
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (i + j == 2) break outer; // jumps fully out of both loops
    }
}
```
The `break outer` transfers control to the point right after the labeled loop. The same trick works with `continue outer` when you want to skip to the next iteration of the outer loop instead. Reach for it only when nesting genuinely demands it — often a `return` from an extracted method reads cleaner.

The clearest way to feel the difference is to watch where execution lands after each keyword. `return` leaves with a value, `continue` skips one item, and `break` drops to the line after the loop.

```java
static Integer findFirstEven(java.util.List<Integer> nums) {
    for (int n : nums) {
        if (n % 2 == 0) return n; // exits the whole method with the result
    }
    return null; // only reached if none found
}

static void processOrders(java.util.List<String> orders) {
    for (String order : orders) {
        if (order.equals("INVALID")) continue; // skip this one
        if (order.equals("STOP")) break;        // stop processing entirely
        System.out.println("Processing: " + order);
    }
    System.out.println("Finished"); // runs after the loop (break lands here)
}
```
The key contrast is the last line: after `break`, `"Finished"` still prints because you only left the loop. Swap that `break` for a `return` and `"Finished"` would never run — the method itself would be gone.

The most common for-each failure is trying to delete from the collection mid-loop. It looks reasonable and compiles fine, then blows up at runtime.

```java
List<String> items = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "bad", "c"));
for (String s : items) {
    if (s.equals("bad")) items.remove(s); // ConcurrentModificationException
}
```
Behind the scenes for-each is driving an `Iterator`, and the iterator keeps a modification counter. Removing through the list directly bumps that counter, the iterator notices the structure changed underneath it, and it throws `ConcurrentModificationException` to stop you from reading corrupted state.

For-each handles the two everyday jobs cleanly: walking a collection and summing an array. The moment you need the position number, though, you switch back to a counted loop.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class ForEachExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var products = List.of("Laptop", "Mouse", "Keyboard");

        // for-each: clean when the index isn't needed
        for (String product : products) {
            System.out.println("Processing: " + product);
        }

        // works with arrays too
        int[] scores = {85, 90, 78, 92};
        int total = 0;
        for (int score : scores) total += score;
        System.out.println("Sum: " + total);

        // when you need the index, use a traditional for
        for (int i = 0; i < products.size(); i++) {
            System.out.println("Index " + i + " = " + products.get(i));
        }
    }
}
```
The last loop is the tell: the only reason it falls back to `i` is that it prints the position. If you ever find yourself maintaining a separate counter inside a for-each, that's a sign you wanted a traditional `for` in the first place.

Here's the classic code-reading trap interviewers use. `day` is 2, so you'd expect only "Tuesday" — but watch what the missing breaks do.

```java
int day = 2;
switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Monday");
    case 2: System.out.println("Tuesday");   // matches here
    case 3: System.out.println("Wednesday"); // falls through and runs
    default: System.out.println("Other");    // also runs
}
// Prints: Tuesday, Wednesday, Other
```
Once `case 2` matches, execution starts there and pours straight down through every label below it, because nothing tells it to stop. The case labels aren't gates — they're just entry points, and `default` is no exception, so it runs too.

Java 14's arrow form makes each case self-contained, so the whole class of bug disappears.

```java
int day = 2;
String name = switch (day) {
    case 1 -> "Monday";
    case 2 -> "Tuesday";
    case 3 -> "Wednesday";
    default -> "Other";
};
System.out.println(name); // Tuesday
```
Classic `switch` needs a `break` in every single case to behave; arrow `switch` breaks implicitly after each one. As a bonus it can be used as an expression that returns a value, which is why this version assigns straight into `name`.

Fall-through isn't always a mistake. Stacking case labels with no code between them is the deliberate way to make several values share one block — here, any weekday maps to the same result.

```java
int day = 6;
String type;
switch (day) {
    case 1: case 2: case 3: case 4: case 5:
        type = "Weekday"; break;  // shared block — intentional grouping
    case 6: case 7:
        type = "Weekend"; break;
    default:
        type = "Invalid";
}
System.out.println(type); // Weekend
// Arrow equivalent: case 6, 7 -> "Weekend";
```
The difference between this and the bug earlier is that there's no code stranded between the stacked labels — they fall through to a single shared statement on purpose. The arrow form expresses the same grouping with a comma list and no risk of a forgotten `break`.
